  <page>
    <title>Modular origami</title>
    <id>246092</id>
    <revision>
      <id>40391788</id>
      <timestamp>2006-02-20T05:58:22Z</timestamp>
      <contributor>
        <ip>165.82.76.201</ip>
      </contributor>
      <comment>/* External links */</comment>
      <text xml:space="preserve">{{cleanup-date|December 2005}}

''Modular Origami'' is a form of [[origami]] that uses multiple small interlocking modules to create a larger model, usually a [[polyhedron]] or other geometric form. Its origins lie with the traditional Japanese [[kusudama]], or medicine ball, in which the modules are strung together with thread to form a sphere that was thought to have several various benevolent properties. Each module requires a separate piece of paper; consequently, models can use anything from three to 10,375 individual sheets (the latter was a modular cat folded by [[Lluis Bigas]])

Most models are fixed together either by inserting preexisting flaps on the module into pockets in the module, or by the use of smaller pieces of paper which are folded and then inserted into pockets to join the modules together, a technique used extensively by [[Tomoko Fuse]] in her book &quot;Unit Origami&quot;

The most common type of Modular Origami depicts geometric forms, such as [[polyhedra]]. For Geometric Modules, each module corresponds to part of a polyhedron; there are modules based on edges, sides, and vertices, although most models will only have one type. These modules can be used to make any polyhedron.

The term ‘Ball Origami’ can also used to describe a few types of modular origami, mostly [[geodesic dome|geodesics]] and other large, irregular shapes. This term, contrary to the belief of many, is not a synonym of Modular origami, as modular origami can also be used to create tessellations of geometric forms and more complex three-dimensional models of other things that do not resemble spheres.

Sometimes other origami models are made with many sheets of paper that have been folded differently, such as many of joseph wu's larger works, but this is usually frowned upon and does not belong to the category of modular origami.

==External links==

* [http://origami.kvi.nl/models/ornament/starkusd/ Star Kusudama Instructions]
* [http://www.ask.ne.jp/~kanzasi/en/e-kusu.html Kusudama Pictures]
* [http://home.comcast.net/~meenaks/diagrams/ Folding Instructions For Many Polyhedra and Variations]
* [http://www.jillsart.com/SectionIndex.aspx?sectionID=modular.origami Some Artistic Examples]

[[Category:Origami]]</text>
    </revision>
